%YAML 1.1
%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 7c7c947f0ccabb24dbf991df8e9f6d4f, type: 3}
  m_Name: factors - social learning
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  defaultQuestions:
  - QuestionText: According to Bandura, which of the following is NOT one of the
      four core processes governing observational learning?
    Options:
    - Attention
    - Retention
    - Reinforcement
    - Motivation
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: 'The characteristic of a model that makes an observer more likely
      to imitate them due to perceived success or status is:'
    Options:
    - Similarity
    - Competence
    - Nurturance
    - Gender
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: 'In social learning theory, the process of remembering the modeled
      behavior is called:'
    Options:
    - Attention
    - Retention
    - Reproduction
    - Motivation
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: Which factor refers to the observer's belief in their own ability
      to perform the modeled behavior?
    Options:
    - Self-efficacy
    - Self-esteem
    - Locus of control
    - Emotional arousal
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: 'When a model is rewarded for a behavior, and this increases the
      observer''s likelihood of imitating that behavior, it is called:'
    Options:
    - Direct reinforcement
    - Vicarious reinforcement
    - Self-reinforcement
    - Punishment
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: 'The cognitive process where an observer must first notice the
      model''s behavior is called:'
    Options:
    - Retention
    - Attention
    - Motivation
    - Reproduction
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: Which of the following is a PERSONAL factor affecting social learning?
    Options:
    - Model's prestige
    - Environmental cues
    - Complexity of the behavior
    - Observer's self-efficacy
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: 'The concept that refers to the match between the model''s and
      observer''s characteristics is:'
    Options:
    - Prestige
    - Similarity
    - Competence
    - Nurturance
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: Which process involves converting stored mental images into overt
      behavior?
    Options:
    - Attention
    - Retention
    - Reproduction
    - Motivation
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: 'The final process in observational learning that determines whether
      learned behaviors will be performed is:'
    Options:
    - Attention
    - Retention
    - Reproduction
    - Motivation
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: 'A student is more likely to imitate a teacher''s problem-solving
      method when the teacher is perceived as:'
    Options:
    - Strict and demanding
    - Knowledgeable and successful
    - Young and inexperienced
    - Distant and formal
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: Which situation best demonstrates the effect of vicarious reinforcement
      on social learning?
    Options:
    - A child receives candy for cleaning their room
    - A student watches another student get praised for asking good questions
    - A teenager practices basketball alone
    - An employee works overtime without recognition
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: A complex behavior is less likely to be learned through observation
      because it affects which process?
    Options:
    - Attention
    - Retention
    - Reproduction
    - Motivation
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: 'A student with high self-efficacy in sports is more likely to:'
    Options:
    - Avoid physical activities
    - Imitate complex athletic skills
    - Focus only on academic subjects
    - Disregard coach's demonstrations
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: Which environmental factor most directly affects the attention
      process in observational learning?
    Options:
    - Cultural values
    - Distractions in the environment
    - Availability of rewards
    - Model's gender
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: A model who is warm and supportive is demonstrating which characteristic
      that enhances observational learning?
    Options:
    - Competence
    - Prestige
    - Nurturance
    - Similarity
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: The ability to mentally rehearse and organize observed behavior
      primarily affects which process?
    Options:
    - Attention
    - Retention
    - Reproduction
    - Motivation
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: 'A student who observes multiple models performing the same behavior
      differently is experiencing:'
    Options:
    - Competing models
    - Consistent modeling
    - Vicarious punishment
    - Direct reinforcement
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: Which factor explains why children are more likely to imitate same-gender
      models?
    Options:
    - Competence
    - Similarity
    - Prestige
    - Nurturance
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: 'The emotional state of the observer affects social learning primarily
      through its impact on:'
    Options:
    - Attention processes
    - Environmental factors
    - Model characteristics
    - Cultural norms
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: Critically analyzing social learning situations, which factor most
      significantly determines whether learned behaviors will be performed?
    Options:
    - The observer's attention to the model
    - The observer's motivation and expected outcomes
    - The model's physical appearance
    - The environmental temperature
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: 'A company wants employees to adopt new safety procedures. Based
      on social learning principles, the MOST effective strategy would be to:'
    Options:
    - Have respected senior employees demonstrate the procedures
    - Post written instructions on bulletin boards
    - Threaten penalties for non-compliance
    - Provide individual training in isolation
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: 'In a diverse classroom, students from different cultural backgrounds
      might respond differently to the same model due to variations in:'
    Options:
    - Biological maturation
    - Cultural values and prior experiences
    - Genetic intelligence
    - Physical abilities
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: Which scenario best illustrates how self-efficacy affects observational
      learning?
    Options:
    - A student watches a math demonstration but doesn't believe they can do it
    - A model receives punishment for their behavior
    - The environment is noisy during demonstration
    - The model is from a different cultural background
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: 'A teacher notices that students imitate behaviors more when they
      see multiple peers performing them. This demonstrates the importance of:'
    Options:
    - Model consistency
    - Model prestige
    - Model similarity
    - Model competence
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: 'Comparing direct reinforcement and vicarious reinforcement, a
      key difference is that vicarious reinforcement:'
    Options:
    - Requires the observer to personally experience consequences
    - Involves observing consequences experienced by others
    - Is always more effective than direct reinforcement
    - Works only with punishment
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: 'A student with low self-efficacy who observes a similar peer succeed
      is likely to experience:'
    Options:
    - Decreased motivation
    - Enhanced self-efficacy and increased effort
    - Complete behavior extinction
    - No change in behavior
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: The phenomenon where violent media exposure leads to increased
      aggression in viewers can be best explained by deficiencies in which process?
    Options:
    - Attention to non-aggressive models
    - Motivation for pro-social behavior
    - Retention of peaceful conflict resolution strategies
    - All of the above
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: 'In organizational settings, the most effective models for promoting
      ethical behavior are typically:'
    Options:
    - High-status leaders who demonstrate ethical conduct
    - Entry-level employees
    - External consultants
    - Automated systems
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: 'A child who observes conflicting behaviors from parents and peers
      is experiencing challenges in:'
    Options:
    - Attention processes
    - Retention processes
    - Motivation processes
    - All observational learning processes
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: Which factor explains why demonstration alone is insufficient for
      learning complex skills like surgery?
    Options:
    - Lack of attention
    - Reproduction difficulties requiring physical practice
    - Insufficient motivation
    - Inadequate retention
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: A teacher who demonstrates multiple ways to solve a problem is
      addressing which factor affecting social learning?
    Options:
    - Model flexibility
    - Behavior complexity
    - Observer self-efficacy
    - Environmental constraints
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: The effectiveness of apprenticeship programs can be largely attributed
      to which combination of factors?
    Options:
    - High model competence + high observer self-efficacy
    - Model similarity + environmental support
    - Competent model + guided reproduction practice
    - Simple behaviors + high motivation
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: 'In multicultural educational settings, social learning is most
      enhanced when:'
    Options:
    - Using only one teaching model
    - Incorporating diverse models that reflect student backgrounds
    - Eliminating all cultural references
    - Focusing only on individual learning
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: A student's past experiences with failure in mathematics primarily
      affects which factor in learning new math strategies?
    Options:
    - Attention to the model
    - Self-efficacy for reproduction
    - Environmental distractions
    - Model characteristics
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: 'The phenomenon where children imitate aggressive behaviors seen
      on television despite knowing they might be punished demonstrates the power
      of:'
    Options:
    - Model prestige and competence
    - Vicarious reinforcement
    - Emotional arousal
    - All of the above
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: 'In workplace training, the most significant barrier to social
      learning is often:'
    Options:
    - Lack of competent models
    - Insufficient opportunities for reproduction
    - Inadequate attention due to distractions
    - Low motivation due to irrelevant content
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: 'A teacher who wants to enhance retention of demonstrated material
      should primarily focus on:'
    Options:
    - Making the demonstration entertaining
    - Providing opportunities for mental rehearsal
    - Increasing the model's prestige
    - Adding environmental distractions
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: 'The relationship between model consistency and observer learning
      can be described as:'
    Options:
    - Inconsistent modeling enhances learning through variety
    - Consistent modeling across multiple models enhances learning
    - Model consistency is irrelevant to learning
    - Inconsistent modeling is always better
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: Which combination of factors would MOST likely result in successful
      social learning?
    Options:
    - Attractive model + complex behavior + distracted observer
    - Nurturant model + irrelevant behavior + low motivation
    - Similar model + impossible behavior + high self-efficacy
    - Competent model + simple behavior + motivated observer
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: 'A student''s cultural background influences social learning primarily
      through its effect on:'
    Options:
    - Biological capacity to learn
    - What behaviors are attended to and valued
    - Physical reproduction abilities
    - Genetic learning predispositions
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: 'In developing educational programs, the most overlooked factor
      in social learning is typically:'
    Options:
    - Model characteristics
    - Observer's self-efficacy
    - Environmental distractions
    - Retention processes
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: The effectiveness of peer tutoring programs can be largely attributed
      to which factor?
    Options:
    - High model prestige
    - Model similarity and relatability
    - Complex behavior demonstration
    - Formal environmental setting
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: 'A manager who wants to promote innovation through social learning
      should emphasize:'
    Options:
    - Punishing failed innovations
    - Rewarding and publicizing successful innovations
    - Keeping innovations secret
    - Discouraging observation of others
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: The digital age has most significantly impacted which factor in
      social learning?
    Options:
    - Availability and diversity of models
    - Biological capacity for attention
    - Genetic learning factors
    - Physical reproduction abilities
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: 'A child who observes parents reading regularly is more likely
      to develop reading habits due to:'
    Options:
    - Direct reinforcement only
    - Vicarious reinforcement and model consistency
    - Genetic predisposition only
    - Environmental pressures only
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: 'In skill acquisition, the transition from observation to performance
      is most dependent on:'
    Options:
    - The observer's motivation and self-efficacy
    - The model's physical appearance
    - Environmental temperature
    - Time of day
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: Which educational approach best addresses multiple factors affecting
      social learning?
    Options:
    - Lecture-only format
    - Independent reading assignments
    - Demonstration + guided practice + feedback
    - Silent meditation
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: 'The most significant limitation of social learning theory is its:'
    Options:
    - Overemphasis on cognitive processes
    - Underestimation of biological constraints
    - Failure to consider environmental factors
    - Ignorance of model characteristics
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - social learning
  - QuestionText: 'In designing anti-bullying programs, the most comprehensive approach
      would address:'
    Options:
    - Only the bullies' behavior
    - Multiple factors including model influences, observer motivation, and environmental
      support
    - Only victim characteristics
    - Only environmental factors
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: factors - social learning
